# Copy to apps/two-two-two/.env and fill in from your Supabase project.
#
# Both apps talk to the SAME Supabase project — one account and one pairing
# serve them both, so apps/intimacy/.env gets the identical two values.
# Pointing them at different projects is not a configuration, it is two
# unrelated couples.
#
# Expo loads this file itself. The root .env is a different thing entirely
# (the test database) and is not read here.

# --- Hosted project --------------------------------------------------------
# Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API.
#   URL       is "Project URL"
#   ANON KEY  is the "anon" / "public" key
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# --- Local stack (`supabase start`) ----------------------------------------
# The CLI prints both on startup; the anon key is the same on every machine.
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<the "anon key" line from `supabase start`>
#
# On a physical device, 127.0.0.1 is the phone. Use your machine's LAN address
# (e.g. http://192.168.1.20:54321) or the app will fail to reach anything.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONLY THE ANON KEY BELONGS HERE.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_ values are compiled into the app bundle, which ships to phones
# and can be read back out of it. That is fine for the anon key: every request
# it makes is still subject to row-level security, which is what actually
# protects the data. Key secrecy is not.
#
# A service_role key bypasses RLS entirely. Putting one in an EXPO_PUBLIC_
# variable would hand every couple's plans, notes, and check-ins to anyone who
# unzips the app. It belongs in Edge Function secrets, never in this file.
#
# This repository is public. `.env` and `.env.*` are gitignored with
# `!.env.example`; never `git add -f` an env file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
